<H1>The New ISFDB Data Entry Rules of Engagement</H1>

<P>
Public data entry has been a part of the ISFDB since it became
widely available in February of 1996. A tremendous amount of effort
has been expended on this feature, both in maintaining the user
interface, as well as correcting the errors made by users while 
entering this data.  However, when one looks at the actual source 
of the bibliographic data currently residing in the ISFDB, it turns 
out that the vast bulk of material has come from sources other than
the public data entry forms. The <i>real</i> source for ISFDB material
has been private databases that individuals have been maintaining on their 
home PC's for years, as well as a small number of large institutional 
databases (such as the NESFA database).
<P>
While much useful information has come from the public data entry
facility, by the end of last year, I was personally spending nearly
every minute I devoted to the ISFDB in repairing bad data collected 
from the data entry interface. The rest of my time was spent answering
angry email about why a particular piece of submitted data wasn't
online yet.
<p>
Although I believe that readers in general
have a higher intellect than non-readers, and that SF readers as a 
whole have a higher intellect than non-SF readers, I am nonetheless
now convinced that the majority of you have absolutely no business
entering data into this database. I have tried this experiment for 
about 4 years now, and I think that I have collected more than enough 
data to prove this assertion. 
<P>
I've reached the end of my rope over people who don't know the difference
between a collection and an anthology. I'm tired of fixing entries where people
entered short stories as anthologies, essays as books of nonfiction, single poems
as entire collections, short stories as novels, magazines without dates of publication, 
and magazine short stories without authors. I've fixed too many submissions that 
were entered backwards with short story titles as authors, and authors as short story titles.
I'm tired of being caught in the middle of the <b>Series Wars</b>: someone will
submit about 85 updates until all of Larry Niven's books are contained in series to 
his/her satisfaction, only to be immediately followed by email from someone else 
who demands to know why Larry Niven's series information is completely brain-damaged.
I'm tired of entering all of the non-SF essays published in non-genre
magazines by marginally-SF authors that no one has ever heard of, when
the bibliographies for major authors like Heinlein, Asimov, and Clarke are
still unfinished.
<p>
We've not really made any progress on the old magazines since January
of 1998, when the deluge of user-submitted data started in earnest. Many
authors were treating the ISFDB as a personal bibliographic service, and
were sending monthly updates on their latest article printed in TV-Guide 
or the local newspaper.  But worst of all, maintaining the ISFDB had 
turned into cut-n-paste drudgery over data which, for the most part,
didn't have wide appeal, especially for the person doing all of the
cutting and pasting.
<p>
So, we're going to re-establish our priorities here, and there
are going to be a few changes. Major changes.
Here then are the new rules of engagement for data that goes into the
ISFDB:
<UL>
<LI>
<B>Author Data</B>: I will leave the author data entry form online. This
is risky - I know. Many errors occur here as well. Not to mention
those people who enter entire bibliographies in that little box
reserved for bios. Nonetheless, a lot of good information is entered
here that cannot be found elsewhere. So it stays. It even has some degree
of idiot-proofing already built in. However, I reserve my editoral right to 
jetisson floatsam. I will immediately file bibliographies submitted in this
manner into the maw of the massive backlog of user-submitted bibliographies. 
<P>
<LI>
<B>Novel Data</B>. Information on novels will come from three areas:
<P>
<OL>
<LI> <I>Forthcoming Books</I>. Forthcoming books eventually become published 
     books, which we verify at the time of publication.
<LI> <I>Book Reviews</I>. We track book reviews of major SF-related
     publications and web sites. The books that are reviewed are entered into the database.
<LI> <I>Archive Data</I>. Information found in various encyclopedias are
     slowly being integrated into the database.
</OL>
<P>
That's it. We are no longer accepting public data on novels at this time.
That means if it's not new, and it's not in an encyclopedia, and it's not 
curently in the database, then it won't be put into the database for the 
near future either. Presence in an encyclopedia also doesn't guarantee that
it will be placed into the database anytime soon.
<P>
<LI>
<B>Magazines</B>. Magazine data will come from two areas:
<P>
<OL>
<LI> From magazines that I buy, or are sent to me.
<LI> Data from people I know, that I trust, and that send me data
   in a format I can use.
</OL>
<P>
That's it. We are no longer accepting public data on magazines
at this time. This has been one of the most error-prone areas
of public data entry. But not <I>the</I> most error-prone.
<P>
<LI>
<B>Collections and Anthologies</B>. <I>This</I> is the most error-prone area of
public data entry. The data received from public data entry for collections
and anthologies is almost never correct. Just like magazines, anthology and 
collection data will come from only two sources:
<P>
<OL>
<LI> From books that I buy, or are sent to me.
<LI> Data from people I trust.
</OL>
<P>
That's it. We are no longer accepting public data on collections and 
anthologies at this time.
<P>
<LI>
<B>Shortfiction, Essay, and Poetry Data</B>: This data will come strictly from magazine,
collection, and anthology data. No loose data will be accepted any more.
<P>
<LI>
<B>Awards Data</B>: No change here. We never allowed the
public to enter this data anyway.
<P>
<LI>
<B>Data Errors</B>: I will accept information on database errors via email.
These must be errors of <I>existing</I> information only; I'm not interested in
errors of omission at this time.
</UL>

So - the upshot of this is that the public can still submit author information,
but nothing else. 

